Against what we already have:
- 0477 EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_GRHAND (Frizti/Fritchii EXP60, vk_rasterizer.cpp:2327-2410): complements,
  does not supersede, no conflict. GRHAND acts before the flush, on the reading thread's lock
  taking in RasterizerVulkan::GetFlushArea: if texture_cache.mutex/buffer_cache.mutex stay taken
  for 256 tries it hands the caches' GetFlushArea to the back and returns the read's pages as
  non-preemptive; the flush itself is unchanged (sync request, the back's Scheduler::Finish under
  the buffer lock, the reader waiting on the sync fence). #1028 acts in the flush: the back
  submits the copies and goes on, the reader waits for the host GPU and lands the bytes. With both
  on: a handed read's queued GetFlushArea answer (in_flight or not) is dropped as before; the
  FlushRegionForRead queued next records no copies for bytes already on their way, and FinishRead
  waits for the download that brings them (one extra request, same result). FinishRead takes
  buffer_cache.mutex on the reading thread once more after the host GPU wait (upstream does too):
  the lock GRHAND avoids, so in GRHAND's convoy scenario a reader can still queue there. No
  textual overlap (this diff adds FlushRegionForRead/FinishRead after FlushRegion and changes
  PrepareRegionWrite).
- 0471 lichii-805-copy-barrier: a transfer-to-later-transfer barrier; #1028's HostReadBarrier is
  a separate memory-write -> host-read barrier on the read downloads only. No overlap.
- 0474 FRITCHII_LISTSYNC (#805's list waits) and 0475 FRITCHII_WRITEMARK: command list waits and
  write marks; #1028 touches neither (MarkWrittenBuffer's new calls take whatever range is marked).
- DMA_SNAPSHOT: see 55601ecbbe above. SEM_FAST: semaphore acquires, not touched.

What each switch does (Common::PeSwitch, exactly "1"; read once):
EDEN_UP_L1028_ASYNC_READBACK=1: GPU::OnCPURead's flush request runs
  RasterizerVulkan::FlushRegionForRead: images download as before (synchronously, as upstream),
  the buffer cache records the downloads of the read's GPU-modified bytes (and, with
  EDEN_NX2_PREFLUSH=0, of the other ranges the guest read before) into a deferred staging buffer,
  records a memory-write -> host-read barrier and submits (BufferCacheRuntime::SubmitForHostRead)
  without waiting. The reading thread then waits for that tick (WaitForHostRead) and writes the
  bytes to guest memory under the buffer lock (BufferCache::FinishReadDownloads); downloads land
  oldest first. Until then the bytes stay GPU-modified; another read of them waits for the same
  download (GetFlushArea: in_flight, also on Preflushable areas) instead of copying again. A GPU
  write recorded over them (MarkWrittenBuffer, SyncValues' copies) keeps them GPU-modified after
  the landing (the read still gets the bytes of its time). Writes to guest memory from the GPU's
  side land the read downloads under them first: MemoryManager::Write/WriteBlock (semaphores,
  inline uploads, DMA, CB data), query results (fence and immediate ones, SemiFlushQueryDirty);
  DMA copies, clears, discarded and inline writes land them (ClearDownload) while guest CPU
  writes and synchronous downloads take their bytes out (ClearReplacedDownload). Fence downloads
  land completed read downloads first (PopAsyncBuffers), older fence downloads drop the bytes a
  read download brings (ClearFenceDownloads), and TickFrame lands those no thread waited for.
  Guest CPU writes collected in a landing's range store their bytes after it
  (DownloadLanding::Await in core/memory.cpp), the buffer cache's downloads (also the synchronous
  ones and the fences') land inside a DownloadLanding, and writes being applied stay listed as
  pending while they are (GPU::GatherAndApply). No staging buffer free: the synchronous path for
  that read.
EDEN_UP_L1028_EARLY=1 (with ASYNC_READBACK): pages whose GPU writes the guest read twice in a row
  get a download recorded right behind each later write (after the dispatch; draws' at
  TickFrame), queued with the next submission (QueueForHostRead), landed when the host GPU ran it
  (after each dispatch, every 32 draws, TickFrame). The read finds the bytes in guest memory or
  waits for that download. Bounded: 64 pages, 1 MiB a frame, 32 pending. Only pages that are not
  Preflushable learn this (a Preflushable read returns before NoteReadBack), so it acts with
  EDEN_NX2_PREFLUSH=0, upstream's mode for Switch 2 titles; with our default preflush it is
  inert. Deferred query results on such pages are noted (NoteReportPending): a GPU write over one
  stays GPU-modified when a download lands it.
EDEN_UP_L1028_DMA_WAIT=1 (needs EDEN_NX2_DMA_SNAPSHOT, the play env has it; not with
  EDEN_NX2_QUERY_DMA_SYNC): a macro DMA read no snapshot covers waits until the back ran the last
  queued entry that may write its pages by the snapshot's notes (compute or copy, unranged
  writer, noted write over the pages; GpuSplit::WaitForNotedWriters, drain site "dma snapshot
  writes"), not the whole ring. If then the buffer cache holds no GPU writes on those pages
  (lock-free skip for pages never GPU-written, else MustFlushRegion under the buffer lock) and the
  query cache lists no report on the words, the words are read from guest memory and the
  snapshot is recorded as after a drain; else the drained read as before.
Diagnostics, no behaviour change unless named:
EDEN_UP_L1028_STATS=1: every 10 s "Lichii #1028 read-back per second (10.0 s; async on|off,
  early on|off, dma wait on|off): guest reads of GPU-written memory S synced on the GPU thread
  (ms), A async (ms), I only waited for a download on its way (ms), longest ms | GPU thread in
  their flushes F ms, buffer cache part B ms | buffer cache host GPU waits under its lock: R for
  reads (ms), O other (ms) | read downloads, early, KiB, fallbacks | readers waited on the host GPU
  N times (ms) | GPU-side writes landed downloads, waited (ms) | landings before writes guest
  memory doesn't hold, waited (ms) | DMA snapshot misses M: narrower wait (ms), drained (ms),
  verify differences | verify: downloads, bytes compared, differ (reader, GPU-side write,
  completed), uncovered, kept not compared". Give it to the control too: there it counts the
  synchronous reads, the back's time in their flushes, its Scheduler::Finish waits under the
  buffer lock split into read flushes and other sync downloads, and the DMA snapshot misses' drain
  time: the basis this port removes.
EDEN_UP_L1028_VERIFY=1 (with ASYNC_READBACK, slow): each read download also copies the read's
  own GPU-modified bytes in the same submission (what a synchronous flush copies) and compares
  guest memory with it as the download lands, leaving out bytes replaced since, guest writes
  still collected, and bytes an earlier download landed in an earlier pass (counted "kept not
  compared"; upstream compares those as they landed, not ported). A read that would only wait for
  a download on its way flushes instead (upstream's check_early): its check, alone when other
  downloads bring all its bytes, lands with them and compares them. First 16 differences logged.
EDEN_UP_L1028_DMA_WAIT_VERIFY=1 (with DMA_WAIT, slow): a narrowed read drains anyway, the macro
  gets the drained words, differences are counted and the first 32 logged.

Deviations from upstream: all behind switches (upstream: always on); counters only with
EDEN_UP_L1028_STATS (upstream: EDEN_NX2_GPU_PROFILE); a read download keeps the bytes of every
collected guest write whatever EDEN_NX2_DOWNLOAD_GUARDS says (the play env sets 0, where our
other downloads skip nothing: a read download lands later than a synchronous flush did, and the
apply of a write it overlaps can land it through CachedWriteMemory -> ClearDownload); an early
download not submitted yet leaves the range a GPU-side write or ClearDownload asks to land
(only a reading thread waits for it): on the split's back the wait would need a submission in the
middle of the work being recorded (e.g. DispatchCompute's indirect DiscardWrite after Configure),
and the fencing thread could wait for a back that waits for it; those bytes stay GPU-modified,
as without early downloads; no staging buffer falls back to the
synchronous flush (TryDownloadStagingBuffer); MayHoldGpuWrites uses our
Tegra::Nx2IsGpuWritten table; the DMA wait is on our snapshot notes (above); VERIFY reduced;
(review) a guest write's landing check (DownloadLanding::Await, a seq_cst fence and a load) runs
only when Core::GPUDirtyMemoryManager::Collect stored new bits, not on every collected write
(reasoning at the call in core/memory.cpp); BufferCacheRuntime::WaitForHostRead, which runs on
reading guest threads, asks GpuSplit::BackRunning rather than the front's own Active flag.

Unset (all switches): T84 behaviour. What still runs: OnCPURead's flush lambda stores a
thread_local bool twice and tests a captured one; one relaxed load of g_read_downloads (always
0) per MemoryManager::Write/WriteBlock page and per query result written; empty-container tests in
ClearDownload/ClearReplacedDownload, GetFlushArea, TickFrame, PopAsyncBuffers, MarkWrittenBuffer
(OutdateReadDownloads, NoteReadBackWrite) and DMACopy; a switch test in NoteWorkRecorded per draw
and dispatch, NoteReadBack per non-preemptive GetFlushArea, GatherAndApply,
ForEachPendingCpuWrite, HandleRasterizerWrite per collected write, SyncValues and the
synchronous downloads' FinishForDownload/landing blocks; early_bytes_this_frame = 0 per frame; new
struct members (RasterizerDownloadArea::in_flight, the buffer cache's read-download state, GPU's
applying_writes). ClearDownload's old body is now ClearFenceDownloads, called by both.
Core::GPUDirtyMemoryManager::Collect returns whether it stored (the result is unused unset). The
generated vk_buffer_cache_cost.cpp gets one more VulkanTimer(7) wait site (WaitForHostRead's
scheduler.Wait), so that anchor message reads 5 instead of 4 wait sites (still skipped as before).

Risks: (1) a write to guest memory from the GPU's side that does not go through MemoryManager or
the query cache (a direct device_memory write) between a read download's record and its landing
loses to the older downloaded bytes (in T84 the synchronous flush landed before such a write);
VERIFY catches what the read itself compares. (2) FinishRead from MemoryManager on the back can
wait for the host GPU (counted "GPU-side writes ... waited"); ClearDownload can too, with the
buffer lock held. (3) SemiFlushQueryDirty lands read downloads with the query cache's mutex held:
a thread holding the buffer lock that then takes the query cache's mutex would deadlock with it
(none found; upstream states the same for its tree). (4) A reading thread waiting for an early
download still queued asks the back to submit (Scheduler::WaitSubmitted, as guest fence waits
do); it holds no cache lock then (EARLY only). (5) FinishRead takes the buffer lock on the reading
thread (GRHAND's convoy).
(6) DMA_WAIT inherits DMA_SNAPSHOT's trust that draws (shader stores) don't write the pages its
reads read, now also for misses, and asks MustFlushRegion under the buffer lock from the front for
pages ever GPU-written (contention with the back). (7) EARLY changes which data reaches guest
memory when (and with EDEN_NX2_PREFLUSH=0 our fence downloads of read ranges stop): upstream's
configuration; on our tree EDEN_NX2_PREFLUSH=0 ran only in a group (t72-q A3, T72: same fps, no
crash), never with EARLY. (8) As upstream, recording a read download cancels the older fence
downloads of its runs (ClearFenceDownloads): from that fence's pop until the read download lands,
another thread that waited for that fence and reads a Preflushable range (no trap there) finds
the bytes from before that fence's writes, where T84's synchronous flush had landed newer ones at
once. Needs two threads on one range within a host GPU round trip; VERIFY does not see it (it
checks the reading thread's own bytes): watch flashes. (9) With ASYNC_READBACK every guest write
that sets new bits in a collected page now pays a seq_cst fence (DownloadLanding::Await), and
every gather a mutex: a cost on the game's threads for a gain that is ~0 with our preflush, so arm
r is the cost check. Never built, never run: syntax-checked only.

Expected (Ocarina, docked full res, full frame wait):
- ASYNC_READBACK alone: -0.2 to +0.1 fps, best guess 0 (the landing checks' cost, risk 9, against
  <= ~3-18 ms/s of back time). Basis: OnCPURead's flush path
  ("CPU read of GPU-written memory ... (N so far)", logged every 64th) runs 2.6-3.6 times/s from
  94 s on in the T83/T84 walk runs (385-449 reads by 115-147 s in every run of batches t83-w to
  t84-d; the pause runs log no 513th read up to their end at 210-230 s: < 1/s in steady play).
  With our default EDEN_NX2_PREFLUSH a range's first read marks it Preflushable and later GPU
  writes there land at fences (T72 profile run430: 2.4-9.6 MB/s in 175-790 copies/s of buffer
  downloads on the back), so only first reads of newly written ranges get here, and
  image pages keep their synchronous texture download. Each such read is at most one
  Scheduler::Finish under the buffer lock on the back (~0.2-5 ms of host GPU, not logged): <= ~3-18
  ms/s of back time, while the back idles 280-390 ms/s (SPLIT_WAITS) and the reading game thread
  waits for the same host GPU work either way (the copies queue behind it). Upstream's +17% came
  from preflush off on Switch 2 titles (every read flushed: 117 ms/s of back fence waits) and no
  frame wait. The control's STATS line gives the real figure: "buffer cache host GPU waits under
  its lock: R for reads (ms)" is what this removes from the back.
- DMA_WAIT: -0.3 to +0.4 fps, best guess 0 to +0.1 (review; was +0.1 to +0.2). Basis: T84 controls
  (a2, a5, b1, b5) show
  "dma snapshot miss" 70-230 ms/s of the front's 400-490 ms/s of waits at 1,100-2,000 misses/s
  (60-150 us each); miss reasons over the run: 67% compute/copy sent since, 17% unranged writer,
  15% noted write, 0.5% new page. A miss now waits only up to its last possible writer, then drains
  anyway if its pages hold GPU writes (unknown share: the drained path flushes whole pages because
  they often do; then it costs the narrower wait, a look under the buffer lock from the front and
  the drain). Upstream's version (draws as writers) did not shorten Ocarina's front wait (235 ->
  250 ms/s). Our own DMA_SNAPSHOT is the warning: t74-d cut the drains 17,137/s -> 6,385/s and the
  total drain time stayed (497 -> 479 ms/s), fps unchanged; LISTSYNC moved list-writer waits into
  acquire waits (+0.6 fps), #949 halved the acquire-check wait (-0.7 fps). Under the full frame
  wait a shorter front wait counts only if back idle falls with it.
- EARLY with EDEN_NX2_PREFLUSH=0 (upstream's shape): -0.5 to +0.5 fps, unknown sign: removes the
  fence downloads of read ranges, adds async flushes for reads early downloads don't cover.

